---
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro'
import HalftoneStack from '@components/art/HalftoneStack.astro'
import DotMatrixMark from '@components/art/DotMatrixMark.astro'
import { SITE } from '@lib/site'
import { MANIFESTO } from '@lib/content'
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="A one-person software studio in Austin, Texas. What I make, how I work, and where AI fits."
>
  <section class="border-b px-6 py-12 lg:px-8 lg:py-16">
    <p class="label text-red-deep">About</p>
    <h1 class="display mt-3 max-w-[14ch] text-[clamp(2.75rem,9vw,6rem)]">
      Small batch, on purpose.
    </h1>
    <p class="label mt-6 max-w-[54ch] text-blue">
      {SITE.company} is a one-person software studio in {SITE.location}, run by{' '}
      <a href="https://markmcdermott.io" class="underline hover:text-red-deep">
        {SITE.author}
      </a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-[1fr_20rem]">
    <div class="border-b px-6 py-10 lg:border-b-0 lg:border-r lg:px-8 lg:py-14">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Most software is built to be sold, then maintained until it can be sold
          again. I make the other kind: small tools that do one thing, that you
          can understand, and that don't need a roadmap to justify themselves.
        </p>

        <h2>How the work happens</h2>

        <p>
          One person, start to finish. Design and engineering aren't handed
          between people here, which means the details that usually get lost in
          translation — the empty state, the keyboard path, the error nobody
          planned for — belong to the same person who drew the happy path.
        </p>

        <p>
          Everything ships small. A tool that's useful at version one is worth
          more than a platform that's impressive at version three and never
          arrives.
        </p>

        <h2>About the machines</h2>

        <p>
          I use AI constantly, and I'd rather say so than let you assume otherwise.
          It writes tests, sketches the boring three-quarters of a component, and
          argues with me about naming. It's the best pair I've ever had at two in
          the morning.
        </p>

        <p>
          What it doesn't do is decide. Every line here has been read by me, and
          anything I couldn't explain to you got deleted rather than shipped. The
          interesting question was never whether a machine can write the code —
          it's who stays accountable for it afterwards. That's still a person.
          It's this one.
        </p>

        <h2>What that rules out</h2>

        <p>
          No accounts where a file would do. No telemetry that serves me rather
          than the person using the thing.{' '}
          <a href="https://mockingboard.design">Mockingboard</a> has no backend at
          all — your images never leave the browser, because there was no good
          reason for them to.
        </p>

        <p>
          An industry that treats attention as a raw material has made a lot of
          software that's very good at being difficult to leave. I'm not
          interested in building that, and I'd rather lose the work than pretend
          engagement and usefulness are the same number.
        </p>

        <p>
          It also rules out volume. There's one pair of hands, so the roster stays
          short on purpose.
        </p>

        <h2>The name</h2>

        <p>
          Small batch, made by someone paying attention, better when it hasn't
          been sitting around: the standards a roaster is held to are not a bad
          set to borrow. The packaging conceit is a joke. The standard isn't.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <aside class="px-6 py-10 lg:px-8 lg:py-14">
      <HalftoneStack class="w-24" />

      <dl class="mt-10 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <dt class="label opacity-45">Studio</dt>
          <dd class="label mt-1">{SITE.company}</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="label opacity-45">Established</dt>
          <dd class="label mt-1">{SITE.established}</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="label opacity-45">Where</dt>
          <dd class="label mt-1">{SITE.location}</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="label opacity-45">Writing</dt>
          <dd class="label mt-1">
            <a href="/press" class="underline hover:text-red-deep">Press</a>
            {' · '}
            <a href="https://markmcdermott.io" class="underline hover:text-red-deep">
              markmcdermott.io
            </a>
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <a
        href="/mailroom"
        class="label mt-10 cta inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-5 py-3"
      >
        Start a conversation
        <span aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
      </a>
    </aside>
  </div>

  <section class="grid border-t lg:grid-cols-[1fr_auto]">
    <div class="px-6 py-10 lg:px-8">
      <h2 class="label">My manifesto</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 space-y-4">
        {MANIFESTO.map((line) => <p class="label max-w-[46ch] text-blue">{line}</p>)}
      </div>
    </div>
    <div
      class="flex flex-col justify-end gap-4 border-t px-6 py-10 lg:border-l lg:border-t-0 lg:px-10"
    >
      <DotMatrixMark class="w-32 text-ink" />
      <p class="label text-red-deep">
        Est. {SITE.established}<br />
        by {SITE.author}
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
